OpenMiles: AIL_open_stream reads Miles' "\\<handle>" stream names

Miles 6.0i's AIL_open_stream takes a file name of the form "\\<n>", n a
Win32 file handle in decimal, and streams from that open handle at its
current position. NOLF's engine streams a long sound stored inside a .rez
archive this way: it opens the archive, seeks to the sound and passes the
handle (lithtech.exe 0x0047dd50, src/engine/lithtech/sndinst2.cpp).
OpenMiles v0.4.0 looked for a file called "\\420" on disk, failed, and the
engine retried every frame, so those sounds never played -- among them the
opening-credits song in T01S01 (Snd\Music\Credits.wav), danced to in
silence. Such a name now reads the RIFF file at the handle's position, as
many bytes as its header says, and plays it from memory; the handle stays
the caller's. Applied by build/ext.sh.

